    mastermind_rules_title: "Mastermind Rules",
    mastermind_rules_intro: "A quick reference for how PaperGames plays Mastermind - useful if you're rusty, or learning.",
    mastermind_rules_basics_title: "The basic idea",
    mastermind_term_code: "The secret code",
    mastermind_rules_code: "At the start of a new game, the computer picks a hidden code of four positions, each set to one of six shapes: circle, square, triangle, diamond, star, or cross. Depending on the difficulty, the same shape may appear more than once in the code.",
    mastermind_term_guess: "Making a guess",
    mastermind_rules_guess: "Tap a slot to cycle it through the six shapes, then tap Submit guess once all four slots show the shapes you want to try. A guess can repeat a shape freely, whether or not the secret code itself has any repeats.",
    mastermind_term_feedback: "Reading the feedback",
    mastermind_rules_feedback: "Each guess gets back a row of pegs: a black peg for every shape that's both correct and in the right position, and a white peg for every shape that's correct but in the wrong position. For example, if the code is circle-circle-square-triangle and you guess circle-square-square-square, you get two black pegs (the first circle and the third square are both exactly right) and no white pegs - the extra squares in the guess have nothing left over in the code to match. The pegs never say which slots they refer to, so working that out from guess to guess is the whole puzzle.",
    mastermind_rules_end_title: "Finishing a game",
    mastermind_term_win: "Winning",
    mastermind_rules_win: "You win the moment a guess earns four black pegs - every shape in exactly the right position.",
    mastermind_term_loss: "Running out of guesses",
    mastermind_rules_loss: "Each difficulty level allows a fixed number of guesses. If you use them all without cracking the code, the game ends there and the secret code is revealed.",
    mastermind_term_levels: "Difficulty levels",
    mastermind_rules_levels: "Easy gives you 12 guesses, but the secret code never repeats a shape. Medium gives 10 guesses and allows repeats, the traditional way the game is played. Hard gives only 8 guesses and also allows repeats, so duplicate shapes are something to watch for.",

    mastermind_history_title: "The History of Mastermind",
    mastermind_history_intro: "A code-breaking game of pure logic, with no rival player to outwit, that grew out of a much older pencil-and-paper guessing game and went on to become a favorite subject for computer scientists studying how to search for a hidden answer efficiently.",
    mastermind_history_origins_title: "From Bulls and Cows to a Board Game",
    mastermind_history_origins_p1: "Mastermind's central mechanic - guess a hidden code and get back only a count of exact and partial matches, never which positions are right - comes from Bulls and Cows, a much older pencil-and-paper (and later computer) guessing game traditionally played with secret numbers instead of shapes or colors.",
    mastermind_history_origins_p2: "In 1970, Mordecai Meirowitz, an Israeli postmaster and telecommunications expert, adapted that idea into a physical board game played with colored pegs. After the concept was turned down by several major toy companies, the British firm Invicta Plastics licensed it and brought it to market that same year under the name Mastermind.",
    mastermind_history_spread_title: "A Best-Seller",
    mastermind_history_spread_p1: "Invicta's Mastermind became one of the best-selling games of the 1970s and 1980s, eventually selling tens of millions of sets worldwide and spawning travel editions, a numbers-only variant, and a long-running series of sequels and spin-offs.",
    mastermind_history_modern_title: "A Favorite Puzzle for Computer Scientists",
    mastermind_history_modern_p1: "Because it boils down to searching for a hidden answer using nothing but match-count feedback, Mastermind became a standard test case in computer science and combinatorics for studying guessing strategies and search algorithms, well beyond its life as a party game.",
    mastermind_history_modern_p2: "In a well-known 1977 paper, \"The Computer as Master Mind\", Donald Knuth showed that the classic four-peg, six-shape version can always be solved in at most five guesses using an optimal strategy - a result still cited whenever the game comes up in discussions of algorithmic search.",
    game_kakuro: "Kakuro",
    home_kakuro_desc: "Fill white cells with digits 1-9 so every run sums to its clue, with no digit repeated within a run. Freshly generated, in three difficulty levels.",

    kakuro_new_game: "New puzzle",
