    // Categories (Stadt, Land, Fluss): rules and history
    categories_rules_title: "Categories Rules",
    categories_rules_intro: "A quick reference for the classic pen-and-paper word game - and for what this page does as your game master.",
    categories_rules_basics_title: "The basic idea",
    categories_term_setup: "Getting ready",
    categories_rules_setup: "Every player needs paper and a pen. Choose the categories together - for example City, Country, River, First name, Animal, Job - and write them as columns across the top of your sheet. Enter the players and the categories on this page, and choose a time limit if you like.",
    categories_term_round: "A round",
    categories_rules_round: "Tap \"Draw a letter\". Everyone now tries to write one word per category that starts with this letter - a city beginning with B, a country beginning with B, and so on. Each letter is drawn only once per game.",
    categories_term_stop: "Stopping",
    categories_rules_stop: "The round ends when the time runs out, or as soon as the first player has filled in every category and calls \"Stop!\" - tap the Stop button then. Everyone puts their pen down immediately.",
    categories_rules_scoring_title: "Scoring",
    categories_term_points: "Points per answer",
    categories_rules_points: "Read the answers out category by category. A unique answer scores 10 points, an answer someone else also wrote scores 5, and if only one player found anything at all for a category, that answer scores 20. A missing or invalid answer scores 0. Tap a cell in the table to cycle through 0, 5, 10 and 20, then tap \"Add up round\".",
    categories_term_disputes: "Doubtful answers",
    categories_rules_disputes: "Whether an answer counts is decided by the group - if in doubt, by a majority vote. Agree before the game whether spelling mistakes or very obscure answers are allowed.",
    categories_term_end: "End of the game",
    categories_rules_end: "Play as many rounds as you like, then tap \"End game\". Whoever has the most points wins.",
    categories_history_title: "The History of Categories",
    categories_history_intro: "A word game played with nothing but paper and pens, known under a different name in almost every country - and with an origin nobody wrote down.",
    categories_history_names_title: "One Game, Many Names",
    categories_history_names_p1: "In German-speaking countries the game is called \"Stadt, Land, Fluss\" after its three classic categories - city, country, river. In France it is \"Petit Bac\", in Italy \"Nomi, cose, città\", in the Netherlands \"Stad, land, rivier\", in Poland \"Państwa-miasta\", and in much of Latin America \"Tutti Frutti\" or \"Basta\". In English-speaking countries it is often simply called \"Categories\" or \"Name, Place, Animal, Thing\".",
    categories_history_names_p2: "The rules are the same everywhere in essence: a random letter, a list of categories, a race against the clock or against the fastest player, and points for answers nobody else thought of. What changes from place to place is mostly the list of categories and how the letter is chosen.",
    categories_history_origin_title: "An Origin Nobody Wrote Down",
    categories_history_origin_p1: "Because the game needs no equipment beyond paper and a pen, it spread from person to person, in schools, families and on long journeys, rather than through a publisher. There is no known inventor and no reliably documented date or place of origin; claims to the contrary are usually anecdotes.",
    categories_history_origin_p2: "What is well established is that it has been a common pastime in many countries for generations, and that commercial boxed versions with printed category cards and dice for the letter appeared much later. PaperGames keeps to the traditional pen-and-paper form and only takes over the game master's jobs: drawing the letter, keeping time and adding up the points.",

